<div class="add-page">
<h1>Add your modules</h1>

<p>It's pretty simple to add yourself to the list of CPAN authors on our front
page. Repository links can point to GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, or another web
host. The supported CI and coverage badges currently use GitHub repositories;
CPAN version and CPANTS badges remain available for other hosts.</p>

<p>You can add your registration using GitHub's web editor. CI services are
optional; CPAN version and CPANTS badges are available without them.</p>
<p>Register your own CPAN account. If you are helping another author, send them
the prepared JSON and these instructions so they can submit their registration.</p>

<h2>Prepare your registration</h2>
<form id="registration-form">
  <div class="mb-3">
    <label for="registration-cpan">CPAN author ID</label>
    <input id="registration-cpan" class="form-control" required pattern="[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*" autocomplete="off">
  </div>
  <div class="mb-3">
    <label for="registration-github">GitHub username (optional)</label>
    <input id="registration-github" class="form-control" autocomplete="off">
  </div>
  <fieldset class="mb-3">
    <legend>Services you already use</legend>
    <label><input id="registration-actions" type="checkbox"> GitHub Actions</label>
    <label><input id="registration-coveralls" type="checkbox"> Coveralls</label>
    <label><input id="registration-codecov" type="checkbox"> Codecov</label>
    <label><input id="registration-cirrus" type="checkbox"> Cirrus CI</label>
  </fieldset>
  <div class="mb-3">
    <label for="registration-workflows">GitHub Actions workflow names (one per line)</label>
    <textarea id="registration-workflows" class="form-control" rows="2" placeholder="CI"></textarea>
  </div>
  <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Generate JSON</button>
  <p id="registration-error" role="alert"></p>
</form>
<div id="registration-result" hidden>
  <p>Create <code id="registration-filename"></code> in the <code>authors</code> directory,
  and paste this JSON into the file:</p>
  <label for="registration-json">Registration JSON</label>
  <textarea id="registration-json" class="form-control" rows="18" readonly></textarea>
</div>
<script src="/js/onboarding.js" defer></script>

<h2>Submit your registration</h2>
<ol>
<li>Sign in to GitHub and open the
<a href="https://github.com/PerlToolsTeam/dashboard/new/master/authors">new-file editor in the authors directory</a>.</li>
<li>Name the file <code>YOURCPANID.json</code> and paste the generated JSON. With JavaScript
 disabled, copy <a href="https://github.com/PerlToolsTeam/dashboard/blob/master/authors/DAVECROSS.json">an existing registration</a>
 and change its author ID, GitHub username, and service settings.</li>
<li>Commit the file and follow GitHub's prompts to propose a pull request. GitHub
can create the fork for you, as described in its
<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/working-with-files/managing-files/creating-new-files">new-file instructions</a>.</li>
<li>After the pull request is merged, the next site refresh gathers your release
metadata and adds your dashboard.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="distribution-settings">Distribution-specific settings</h2>

<p>The <code>ci</code> object in <code>authors/YOURCPANID.json</code> supplies the
defaults for all your distributions. If they use different services or workflow
names, add a <code>distribution_ci</code> object alongside <code>author</code> and
<code>ci</code>. Its keys are CPAN distribution names, such as
<code>Example-Dist</code>, rather than module names, repository names, or release
names with a version number.</p>

<p>For example, this registration tracks a workflow named <code>CI</code> and
Coveralls by default. <code>Example-Dist</code> instead tracks the workflow file
<code>test.yml</code> and disables Coveralls; <code>Example-Legacy</code> disables
GitHub Actions while keeping Coveralls:</p>

<pre><code class="language-json">{
  "author": {
    "cpan": "EXAMPLE",
    "github": "example"
  },
  "ci": {
    "use_gh_actions": 1,
    "gh_workflow_names": ["CI"],
    "use_coveralls": 1
  },
  "distribution_ci": {
    "Example-Dist": {
      "gh_workflow_names": [],
      "gh_workflow_files": ["test.yml"],
      "use_coveralls": 0
    },
    "Example-Legacy": {
      "use_gh_actions": 0
    }
  }
}</code></pre>

<ul>
<li>Settings you omit inherit their values from <code>ci</code>.</li>
<li>Lists replace the entire inherited list. Use <code>[]</code> to clear one.
For example, clear <code>gh_workflow_names</code> when switching to filenames
to avoid showing both sets of badges.</li>
<li>Set a service's <code>use_*</code> flag to <code>1</code> to show its badges,
or <code>0</code> to hide them. Disabling a service keeps the distribution in
your dashboard.</li>
<li>Any setting supported in <code>ci</code> can also go in a distribution's
override. Changes take effect on the next site build, including builds using
cached release metadata.</li>
</ul>

<p>The form above generates author-level defaults. Edit the generated JSON before
submitting it to add distribution overrides, workflow filenames, Cirrus task
names, or AppVeyor settings. For an existing registration, edit your file in the
<a href="https://github.com/PerlToolsTeam/dashboard/tree/master/authors">authors directory</a>
and propose a pull request.</p>

<h2>Supported CI services</h2>

<p>These settings control the badges on your dashboard. Set up each service in
your code repository first, then enable its badges in your registration. The
JSON settings below can go in your author-level <code>ci</code> object or in one
distribution's <code>distribution_ci</code> override.</p>

<div id="accordion">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header" id="headingOne">
      <h3 class="mb-0">
        <button class="btn btn-link" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseOne"
                aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="collapseOne">
          GitHub Actions
        </button>
      </h3>
    </div>
    <div id="collapseOne" class="collapse show" aria-labelledby="headingOne" data-bs-parent="#accordion">
      <div class="card-body">
        <p>GitHub Actions can do many things. But we're just using it to run tests on each commit
          and report on the success or failure of the test run. Add a directory called
          <tt>.github/workflows</tt> to your code repository and in that directory create a YAML
          file that runs the processes that you want to run. A basic workflow file for testing a
          CPAN module might look
          <a href="https://github.com/davorg/array-compare/blob/master/.github/workflows/perltest.yml">something like this</a>.</p>
        <p>You can track as many GitHub Actions as you like and the badges will stack on top of
          each other in your dashboard (this isn't currently as pretty as I'd like it, but we're
          working on it). Remember the values of the <tt>name</tt> keys in your YAML files as you'll
          need to add those to the <tt>ci.gh_workflow_names</tt> option in your new configuration
          file. Alternatively, list YAML filenames such as <tt>test.yml</tt> in
          <tt>ci.gh_workflow_files</tt>. Use just the filename, without the
          <tt>.github/workflows/</tt> directory, and set <tt>use_gh_actions</tt> to 1.</p>
        <p>If repositories use different workflows, use the
          <a href="#distribution-settings">distribution-specific settings</a> above.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header" id="headingThree">
      <h3 class="mb-0">
        <button class="btn btn-link" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseThree"
                aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="collapseThree">
          Coveralls.io
        </button>
      </h3>
    </div>
    <div id="collapseThree" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="headingThree" data-bs-parent="#accordion">
      <div class="card-body">
        <p><a href="https://coveralls.io/">Coveralls.io</a> measures your test coverage.  Log in
          with your Github credentials and turn on the repos that you want to use. You also have to
          find a way to feed coverage data to your Coveralls.io account. For example, using GitHub
          Actions, I define a job like this:</p>
        <pre>
  coverage:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    container: davorg/perl-coveralls:latest
    name: Test coverage
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - name: Install modules
        run: cpanm -n --installdeps .
      - name: Coverage
        env:
          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
        run: cover -test -report Coveralls
        </pre>
        <p>Next time you commit something to one of your repos, you will also get coverage
          information appearing at Coveralls.io.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header" id="headingFive">
      <h3 class="mb-0">
        <button class="btn btn-link" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseFive"
                aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="collapseFive">
          Codecov.io
        </button>
      </h3>
    </div>

    <div id="collapseFive" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="headingFive" data-bs-parent="#accordion">
      <div class="card-body">
        <p><a href="https://codecov.io/">Codecov</a> displays test coverage from
          reports uploaded by your CI workflow. Sign in with GitHub, select your
          repository, and copy its upload token into a GitHub Actions repository
          secret named <code>CODECOV_TOKEN</code>. The token belongs in your CI
          secrets, rather than your dashboard registration.</p>
        <p>For a Perl distribution, <a href="https://metacpan.org/pod/Devel::Cover::Report::Clover">Devel::Cover::Report::Clover</a>
          produces a <a href="https://docs.codecov.com/docs/supported-report-formats">supported Clover XML report</a>.
          Add these steps to an existing GitHub Actions job after checking out
          your code, setting up Perl, and installing the distribution's dependencies:</p>
        <pre><code class="language-yaml">- name: Generate coverage
  run: |
    cpanm --notest Devel::Cover Devel::Cover::Report::Clover
    cover -test -report clover
- uses: codecov/codecov-action@v5
  with:
    files: cover_db/clover.xml
    token: ${{ secrets.CODECOV_TOKEN }}
    fail_ci_if_error: true</code></pre>
        <p>See the <a href="https://github.com/codecov/codecov-action">official Codecov action instructions</a>
          for authentication options and further settings. After a successful
          upload on your default branch, check that Codecov shows coverage for
          that commit, then enable the dashboard badge:</p>
        <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "use_codecov": 1
}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header" id="headingSix">
      <h3 class="mb-0">
        <button class="btn btn-link" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseSix"
                aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="collapseSix">
          Cirrus CI
        </button>
      </h3>
    </div>

    <div id="collapseSix" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="headingSix" data-bs-parent="#accordion">
      <div class="card-body">
        <p><a href="https://cirrus-ci.com/">Cirrus CI</a> runs tasks defined in
          <code>.cirrus.yml</code> at the root of your repository. Install the
          <a href="https://github.com/apps/cirrus-ci">Cirrus CI GitHub app</a>
          and give it access to the repositories you want to test, following
          its <a href="https://github.com/cirruslabs/cirrus-ci-docs/blob/master/docs/guide/quick-start.md">quick-start guide</a>.</p>
        <p>For a distribution with tests in <code>t</code> and modules in
          <code>lib</code>, a starting configuration is:</p>
        <pre><code class="language-yaml">test_task:
  name: Tests
  container:
    image: perl:5.40
  install_script: cpanm --installdeps --notest .
  test_script: prove -l t</code></pre>
        <p>Choose a Perl version your distribution supports and use your usual
          build and test commands if it needs compilation or generated files.
          Commit the file and confirm that the <code>Tests</code> task succeeds
          on Cirrus CI. Then add both the service flag and the task names to your
          dashboard settings:</p>
        <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "use_cirrus": 1,
  "cirrus_task_names": ["Tests"]
}</code></pre>
        <p>Use the exact displayed task names, including capitalization. The
          dashboard shows one badge per name; <code>use_cirrus</code> alone does
          not produce a task badge. The form sets the flag, so add
          <code>cirrus_task_names</code> manually. See the
          <a href="https://github.com/cirruslabs/cirrus-ci-docs/blob/master/docs/guide/writing-tasks.md">task and badge documentation</a>
          for more options.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header" id="headingSeven">
      <h3 class="mb-0">
        <button class="btn btn-link" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseSeven"
                aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="collapseSeven">
          AppVeyor
        </button>
      </h3>
    </div>

    <div id="collapseSeven" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="headingSeven" data-bs-parent="#accordion">
      <div class="card-body">
        <p><a href="https://www.appveyor.com/">AppVeyor</a> can test your Perl
          distribution on Windows. Sign in with GitHub, create a project for
          your repository, and add <code>appveyor.yml</code> at its root. This
          example uses Strawberry Perl on the Visual Studio 2022 image:</p>
        <pre><code class="language-yaml">image: Visual Studio 2022
build: off
install:
  - set "PATH=C:\Strawberry\perl\bin;C:\Strawberry\perl\site\bin;C:\Strawberry\c\bin;%PATH%"
  - perl -V
  - cpanm --installdeps --notest .
test_script:
  - prove -l t</code></pre>
        <p>The PATH setting selects Strawberry Perl ahead of other installed
          Perls. <code>build: off</code> skips AppVeyor's default build step;
          <code>test_script</code> still runs. Adjust the commands if your
          distribution needs a build step. See the
          <a href="https://www.appveyor.com/docs/windows-images-software/">installed software list</a>
          and <a href="https://www.appveyor.com/docs/appveyor-yml/">YAML reference</a>
          for supported images and settings.</p>
        <p>Commit the file and confirm a successful build on AppVeyor, then
          add this setting manually to your registration; the form has no
          AppVeyor checkbox:</p>
        <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "use_appveyor": 1
}</code></pre>
        <p>The dashboard labels this badge <code>Windows</code> and constructs
          its project link from the GitHub repository's owner and name. It
          currently has no setting for a different AppVeyor account or project
          slug.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="repository-metadata">Repository metadata</h2>

<p>A distribution stays in the dashboard when its repository link is absent or
unsupported. Add a web URL under <code>resources.repository.web</code> in the
distribution metadata, then publish a new release. Links to other web hosts are
retained; the supported service badges require a recognized GitHub repository.</p>

<h2>Fixing issues</h2>

A number of issue that could be discovered by the CPAN Dashboard and how to fix them.

<ul>
<li>Add a link to the code repos to the metadata of all of your CPAN modules.
(Gabor has <a href="https://perlmaven.com/how-to-add-link-to-version-control-system-of-a-cpan-distributions"> a tutorial about that</a>.)</li>
<li>Configure GitHub Actions</li>
<li>Configure Coveralls</li>
<li>...</li>
</ul>

</div>
